{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB11264 - MONDO_1567",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Glycerophosphate is associated with Nephrocalcinosis through a chain of indications that link it to Calcium Acetate, which in turn is linked to the condition. The evidence suggests that Calcium Glycerophosphate may contribute to Nephrocalcinosis via its role in phosphorus metabolism, as it is indicated to affect Phosphorus Metabolism Disease, which is linked to Calcium Acetate. This pathway implies a potential mechanism where the metabolic effects of Calcium Glycerophosphate could influence calcium homeostasis, leading to the formation of calcium deposits in the kidneys, as seen in Nephrocalcinosis. The presence of multiple indirect pathways, all leading through Calcium Acetate, supports a broader role for Calcium Glycerophosphate in the development of this condition. The integration of these pathways highlights a possible dependency on phosphorus metabolism and calcium regulation, which may be a key factor in the pathogenesis of Nephrocalcinosis.",
